FFmpeg's DASH demuxer returns 0, "a packet was read", from dash_read_packet when it is interrupted before it read one: the
loop that reads (while (!ff_check_interrupt(...) && !ret)) ends on the interrupt with ret still 0, and the function
returns ret. libavformat takes 0 for a packet (an empty one), reads again, and gets the same for as long as the interrupt
lasts: a loop in av_read_frame at 100% of a core that only the end of the interrupt breaks. A user of the demuxer that
interrupts a read to take the input from it (a seek) and holds the interrupt until it has the input waits for good: three
times in four within a hundred and fifty seeks, in Nits' tests. (A cancel followed by a close met it in none of four
hundred tries: a read cut short inside a segment ends with an error; it takes the interrupt between two segments.) Other
demuxers return AVERROR_EXIT (or the error of the I/O that failed) for an interrupted read, as the HLS demuxer does.
Upstream master (libavformat/dashdec.c as of 2026-11-07) has the loop as it is in 9.0.2; not reported there
yet. Applied by scripts/build_dependencies.sh to FFmpeg 9.0.2.

--- a/libavformat/dashdec.c
+++ b/libavformat/dashdec.c
@@ -2409,7 +2409,9 @@
             return FFERROR_REDO;
         }
     }
-    return ret;
+    /* The loop also ends when interrupted, with nothing read and ret still 0: 0 says a packet was read, and libavformat
+     * (ff_read_packet) asks again, for as long as the interrupt lasts. */
+    return ret ? ret : AVERROR_EXIT;
 }
 
 static int dash_close(AVFormatContext *s)
